        <!-- ABSTRACT -->
        <section id="abstract">
            <div class="section-header">§ 01 — Abstract</div>
            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h2 style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Organic-Walled Hydrozoans as Paleoenvironmental Sentinels</h2>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">The discovery of organic-walled hydrozoan microfossils in the Bächental Basin of the Austrian Alps provides crucial insights into the paleoenvironmental conditions during the Early Jurassic Toarcian Oceanic Anoxic Event (T-OAE, ~183 Ma).<sup>[3]</sup> This report defines the morphology and taxonomy of these hydrozoan remains — including polypoid, medusoid, and incertae sedis forms — and correlates them with similar discoveries across the northwestern Tethyan realm.<sup>[4][5]</sup></p>
                    <p>By applying the palynofacies framework pioneered by Fonseca et al. (2018), the relative abundance of polypoid versus medusoid forms is interpreted as a sensitive proxy for bottom-water oxygenation and basin restriction.<sup>[4]</sup> The Bächental specimens demonstrate that hydrozoan zoomorphs were widespread across the Tethys during the T-OAE and represent a powerful tool for high-resolution paleoenvironmental reconstruction.</p>
                    
                    <div class="tag-list">
                        <span>T-OAE</span> <span>Hydrozoa</span> <span>Palynofacies</span> <span>Zoomorphs</span> <span>Anoxia</span> <span>Tethys</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <div class="image-card">
                        <div class="image-placeholder">
                            [Figure 25: Microscope images of Polypoid, Medusoid and Incertae sedis forms]
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Figure 25.</strong> C) Hydrozoan polypoid form. Organic-walled microfossil showing complex, branching morphology characteristic of the polyp stage. A-B) Hydrozoan medusoid forms. A) Circular to sub-circular organic remains exhibiting typical umbrella-like compression. B) Fragmentary medusoid specimen showing the membrane structure. D) Incertae sedis. Elongated, filamentous organic structure with a terminal cluster.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 style="margin-top: 4rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Geological Context</h3>
