        <!-- MORPHOLOGY -->
        <section id="morphology">
            <div class="section-header">§ 02 — Morphology</div>
            <h2 style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Morphological Description of Specimens</h2>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">Hydrozoans exhibit a complex life cycle alternating between a benthic sessile polyp stage and a pelagic free-swimming medusa stage.<sup>[9]</sup> In palynological preparations, these organisms are preserved as organic-walled microfossils — "zoomorphs" — whose morphology reflects the original biological structure of each life stage.<sup>[7]</sup></p>
            
            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="orange" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">Polypoid Form — Sessile Colonial Hydroid</h3>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">The polypoid form (Figure 25C) is characterized by a complex, branching morphology with irregular, jagged outlines. This organic-walled microfossil represents the preserved chitinous perisarc (exoskeleton) that surrounds the hydrocaulus (stem) and hydranths (feeding polyps) of colonial hydroids.<sup>[9]</sup></p>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">The branching pattern reflects the colonial architecture of the original organism, with multiple zooid chambers visible as darker, more opaque regions within the translucent chitinous framework. The preservation of these delicate structures indicates low-energy depositional conditions and rapid burial under reducing conditions.<sup>[6]</sup></p>
                    
                    <div class="card">
                        <table style="margin-top:0;">
                            <tr>
                                <th style="color:var(--accent-orange)">MORPHOMETRIC DATA</th>
                                <th></th>
                            </tr>
                            <tr><td>Size</td><td>~40–80 µm (estimated)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Wall composition</td><td>Chitinous perisarc</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Branching</td><td>Dichotomous to irregular</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Opacity</td><td>Moderate to high (brown)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td style="border:none;">Scale bar</td><td style="border:none;">20 µm (Fig. C)</td></tr>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="pie-chart-container">
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 2rem; align-self: flex-start;">Relative Abundance of Morphotypes</h3>
                    <div class="pie-chart"></div>
                    <div style="display:flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; font-size: 0.85rem;">
                        <span style="color:var(--accent-teal)">■ Medusoid (35%)</span>
                        <span style="color:var(--accent-orange)">■ Polypoid (45%)</span>
                        <span style="color:#6366f1">■ Incertae Sedis (12%)</span>
                        <span style="color:#475569">■ AOM (8%)</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- TAXONOMY -->
        <section id="taxonomy">
            <div class="section-header">§ 03 — Taxonomy</div>
            <h2 style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Systematic Classification</h2>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">The taxonomic classification of these organic-walled microfossils places them within the animal kingdom, specifically as cnidarian zoomorphs. In palynofacies analysis, these remains are classified under the Zoomorph Subgroup of the Zooclast Group.<sup>[7]</sup></p>
            
            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Formal Taxonomic Hierarchy</h3>
                    <div class="card" style="padding:0;">
                        <table style="margin-top:0;">
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main)">KINGDOM</th><td>Animalia</td></tr>
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main)">PHYLUM</th><td><em>Cnidaria</em> Hatschek, 1888</td></tr>
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main)">CLASS</th><td><em>Hydrozoa</em> Owen, 1843</td></tr>
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main)">SUBCLASS</th><td><em>Hydroidolina</em> Marques & Collins, 2004</td></tr>
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main)">ORDERS</th><td><em>Anthoathecata / Leptothecata</em> (Probable affinities)</td></tr>
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main)">PALYN. GROUP</th><td>Zooclast Group</td></tr>
                            <tr><th style="color:var(--text-main); border:none;">PALYN. SUBGROUP</th><td style="border:none;">Zoomorph Subgroup</td></tr>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Taxonomic Determination Notes</h3>
                    <div class="card proxy-card proxy-teal">
                        <h4 class="teal">Class-Level Assignment</h4>
                        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">Assignment to Class Hydrozoa is supported by the presence of both polypoid and medusoid life stages, the chitinous composition of the perisarc, and the overall morphology consistent with hydroid colonies and their medusae.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card proxy-card proxy-orange">
                        <h4 class="orange">Subclass Hydroidolina</h4>
                        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">The alternating polyp-medusa life cycle and the chitinous perisarc structure suggest affinities with Hydroidolina, which includes the orders Anthoathecata (athecate hydroids) and Leptothecata (thecate hydroids). Both are known from the Jurassic fossil record.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card proxy-card proxy-gray">
                        <h4 style="color:#6366f1">Limitations & Palynofacies</h4>
                        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">Specific assignment to lower taxonomic levels is challenging due to the fragmentary nature. In the palynofacies framework, these remains belong to the Zoomorph Subgroup.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- PALEOENVIRONMENT -->
        <section id="paleoenvironment">
            <div class="section-header">§ 04 — Paleoenvironment</div>
            <h2 style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Paleoenvironmental Significance</h2>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">The relative abundance of polypoid versus medusoid forms provides a proxy for benthic oxygenation and basin restriction, as established by Fonseca et al. (2018) — the first study to apply hydrozoans as paleoenvironmental indicators in palynofacies analysis.<sup>[4]</sup></p>

            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Hydrozoan Oxygenation Proxy Model</h3>
                    <div class="card proxy-card proxy-orange">
                        <h4 class="orange">POLYPOID DOMINANCE → OXYGENATED</h4>
                        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">High abundance of sessile benthic polypoid forms indicates a well-oxygenated proximal water body with strong marine influence. Polyps require oxygenated bottom waters to survive.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card proxy-card proxy-teal">
                        <h4 class="teal">MEDUSOID DOMINANCE → DYSOXIC/ANOXIC</h4>
                        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">Dominance of amorphous zoomorphs (free-swimming medusoid forms) indicates a restricted, stagnated environment with dysoxic to anoxic bottom waters and strong stratification.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card proxy-card proxy-gray">
                        <h4 style="color:#6366f1">MIXED ASSEMBLAGE → TRANSITIONAL</h4>
                        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem;">Mixed assemblages, as observed in the Bächental Basin, reflect oscillating redox conditions controlled by sea-level fluctuations and restricted water mass exchange.<sup>[3]</sup></p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div style="display:flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;">
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem; align-self:flex-start;">Paleoenvironmental Indicator Profile</h3>
                    <!-- Simple CSS Radar Chart representation via SVG -->
                    <svg width="300" height="300" viewBox="0 0 100 100" style="background:var(--bg-card); border-radius:50%; padding: 10px;">
                        <!-- Web lines -->
                        <polygon points="50,10 90,30 90,70 50,90 10,70 10,30" fill="none" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.1)" stroke-width="0.5"/>
                        <polygon points="50,25 75,40 75,60 50,75 25,60 25,40" fill="none" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.1)" stroke-width="0.5"/>
                        <!-- Axes -->
