        <!-- DISCUSSION -->
        <section id="discussion">
            <div class="section-header">§ 06 — Discussion</div>
            <h2 style="font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Discussion</h2>
            
            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">Taphonomy and Preservation</h3>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">The preservation of hydrozoan soft tissues as organic-walled microfossils in the Bächental marls is exceptional. Hydrozoan medusae lack biomineralized hard parts and are highly susceptible to decay under normal aerobic conditions.<sup>[6]</sup> Their preservation in the T-OAE sediments is facilitated by the anoxic conditions and rapid organic matter burial characteristic of the event.<sup>[8]</sup></p>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">The pelagic medusoid forms likely inhabited the oxygenated surface waters (epipelagic zone) and sank into the anoxic, hydrogen sulfide-rich bottom waters upon death, where their organic remains were preserved from scavenging and aerobic decay.</p>
                    
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1rem; margin-top: 2rem;">Biological Response to the T-OAE</h3>
                    <p>While the T-OAE devastated calcareous marine invertebrates and nannoplankton due to ocean acidification and anoxia,<sup>[10]</sup> organic-walled organisms like hydrozoans and prasinophyte algae appear to have thrived or at least preserved preferentially in the stratified, organic-rich environments.<sup>[4]</sup></p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">Implications for Bächental Basin Reconstruction</h3>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">In the context of the Bächental Basin, the presence of both polypoid and medusoid forms reflects the dynamic and heterogeneous nature of the basin during the T-OAE. Geochemical proxies indicate that the basin experienced varying suboxic to euxinic conditions, controlled by sea-level fluctuations.<sup>[3]</sup></p>
                    <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">The presence of benthic polypoid fragments may indicate either transport from shallower, oxygenated basin margins into the deeper anoxic depocenter, or intermittent periods of bottom-water oxygenation during relative sea-level lowstands.</p>
                    
                    <div class="key-conclusion">
                        <h4>KEY CONCLUSION</h4>
                        <p>"The Bächental hydrozoan assemblage confirms that these organic-walled cnidarian remains are widespread paleoenvironmental indicators across the Tethyan domain during the T-OAE, with the polypoid/medusoid ratio serving as a sensitive proxy for bottom-water oxygenation."</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

